// Proactive English C1 · Syria Edition · Book 1 · Unit 3 — Answer Key
const L = require('./lib');
const { p, h1, h2, pb, sp, box, table, C } = L;
const out = [];
const S = (t) => p(t, { bold: true, color: C.teal, before: 160, after: 60 });
const A = (t) => p(t, { indent: 200, after: 50 });
const SAMPLE = (paras) => box('Sample answer', paras, 'FFFFFF', C.navy);
const NOTE = (t) => box('Note', [t], C.lyellow, C.orange);

out.push(h1('Unit 3  Choosing What Not to Say — Answer Key'));
out.push(p('_Proactive English · C1 · Book 1 · Syria Edition · Answer Key_', { color: '7F7F7F' }));
out.push(box('How to use this key', [
  'Every task has an answer or a **full sample**. **No model in this book contains a sentence about its own writing**, and the covering test from 1.6 is the class\'s own correction for it.',
  '⚠ **This unit teaches one decision twice, and it is the only unit in Book 1 that does.** **Speaking Part 2 gives three photographs and asks for two.** **Writing Part 1 gives three points and asks for two.** **They are in the same unit because they are the same decision**, and the class should be told so in 3.1 and reminded in 3.5 — **one student in the room was taught the two tasks by two different teachers and neither of them mentioned it.**',
  '**What to collect before 3.1 and chase.** **Something each student decided not to say this year, in writing, at work — and whether anybody noticed.** ⚠ **The second half of that is the whole of the cycle**, and a class that arrives with only the first half will discuss honesty instead of learning a technique.',
  '**3.7 carries the examination papers.** **Reading and Use of English Part 1** is the multiple-choice cloze, drawing on this unit\'s phrasal verbs; **Writing Part 1** is the essay; **Listening Part 3** is a long interview with four options per question.',
  '⚠ **And the distinction to hold on to, because the unit turns on it and it is not about honesty.** **3.1 declares the omission because the reader does not know the thing exists.** **3.3 says nothing about the third photograph because the examiner put it there.** **The rule is about who set the limit** — **declare the choice to whoever did not set it** — **and 3.5 is the writing version of the same test.**',
], C.lblue, C.navy));

// ===================== 3.1 =====================
out.push(pb());
out.push(h2('3.1  Two of the three'));
out.push(S('Stage 1 · Get ready'));
out.push(A('**a** Open. ⚠ **Enforce the two-word answer: _noticed_ or _not noticed_, and no explanation of the decision.** **The explanations will come and they are the wrong material** — this is not a cycle about discretion. **Count the two columns and put the figures up.** **In most classes seven or eight were not noticed**, which is the number the rest of the unit rests on.'));
out.push(A('**b** Declared omissions: **2** and **5**. ⚠ **Not 1, 3 or 4.** **Read 4 aloud** — _space does not permit a full treatment of the subject_ — **and ask what is missing.** **Nobody can answer**, which is the definition: **a formula names nothing and is a silent omission with a sentence in front of it.** **3 is the subtle one**: _I shall confine myself to the two main points_ **names a number and not a thing, and _main_ is doing the same work as _important_ in 3.5.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 2 · Listen and read'));
out.push(A('**a** **B** — **a declared omission names the thing left out and gives one reason; a formula names nothing.**'));
out.push(A('**b** **1** ⚠ **Three strands at about twenty-six words each, which is not enough for any of them** — **and the desk cut the dispute, and the cut version is better, and he has been annoyed for three weeks because the desk chose and he had not.** **2** **The instruction says _discuss two of the following three points_**, and she was taught that covering three shows range. ⚠ **Her band scores were content three out of five with nothing else below four.**'));
out.push(NOTE('⚠ **Mrs Ajouz\'s band scores are the most useful piece of evidence in Book 1 and they should be written on the board: content 3, nothing else below 4.** **The class will try to read that as a mark for weak English and it is not** — **every other criterion says her English was fine.** **The three was for not following an instruction**, and the instruction was to choose. **Then the arithmetic beside it: eighty words divided by three, or a minute divided by three, gives twenty-six words or twenty seconds, and neither will carry a figure with a source and a date.** ⚠ **Indecision and modesty produce identical prose**, and an examiner cannot tell them apart and does not try.'));
out.push(A('**The limit of this cycle is in Stage 3R and it matters more than the technique, because a class given a licence to leave things out will over-apply it by Thursday.** ⚠ **You can choose between topics and you cannot choose between the parts of one fact.** **A dose without a patient and a date is not a dose; a figure without a denominator and a date is not a figure.** **Four rows on the practice cards are exactly that trap and the class has to find them.** ⚠ **Put the test on the board: if the three things are all needed to make one of them true, you have one fact and not three topics.** **It returns in Unit 6 as the cut that removes words and not facts, and in Unit 19 as the sentence that cannot be read two ways.**'));
out.push(S('Language box · Practise'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**1** _(three faults, two reported)_ _The seal was broken on two cartons and the temperature log has an eleven-hour gap. **The third fault, a missing signature on the delivery note, is not in this report, because the note is the depot\'s document and not ours.**_',
  '**2** _(three reasons, two given)_ _We cannot certify it: the bolts are M12 against a specified M16, and nine positions are unmeasured. **I have not raised the mill certificates, because the certificate is withheld on the first two grounds in any case.**_',
  '**3** _(three options, two costed)_ _Nine months in one district or twelve in two. **I have not costed the third option, because the second is already cheaper than doing nothing.**_',
  '**4** _(three findings, two written up)_ _The eleven days; the unread district column. **The third finding, about the vehicle, is a management matter and is in the field office\'s report and not in this one.**_',
  '**5** _(the one left out is the most interesting)_ ⚠ _The ordering dispute is the most interesting of the three and it is the one that cannot be done in twenty-six words. **It is disputed between the directorate and the municipality and is not in this piece.**_',
]));
out.push(A('⚠ Mark these on **the reason and not on the omission.** **The reason has to be about the thing** — _the note is the depot\'s document_ · _the second is already cheaper_ — **and not about the page.** **Item 5 is the one to read twice**: ⚠ **it declares that the omitted strand is the most interesting one and leaves it out anyway**, which is the only version of this move that a reader believes.'));
out.push(S('Stage 3 · Practise (sample – Card A)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**A** _(two of three, forty seconds)_ The main was closed on three mornings in June and about four hundred households were affected. The work cost about eleven million pounds, a third of the quarterly budget, and the directorate confirmed the figure on Tuesday.',
  '**A** _(the declared omission)_ **The order for the work is disputed and is not in this piece, because it needs two claims and two documents and there is no honest version of it in twenty-six words.**',
  '**B** Left out: **who ordered it.** Declared: **yes.** Reducible: **yes.** ⚠ _(the second row)_ **The dose, the patient and the date: not reducible — the three are one fact.**',
]));
out.push(S('Stage 3R · Perform – listener task'));
out.push(A('The omissions and whether each was declared. ⚠ **The four marked rows cannot be reduced, and the room should produce the reason rather than be given it.** **Ask of each: if I leave one out, is either of the others still true?** **A dose with no date is not a dose that happened on an unknown day; it is not a dose at all.** **Then put the two categories on the board and leave them up for the unit** — **topics, which can be chosen between, and parts of one fact, which cannot.** ⚠ **Expect one student to argue that the ordering dispute in Mr Shihabi\'s piece is a part of the fact and not a topic.** **It is a good argument and it is wrong: the closure is true whether or not anybody knows who ordered it.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 4 · Your turn (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**a** _**Kept:** the eleven unrecorded dispensings; the superseded sheets. **Dropped:** the locum\'s contract, which expires in January._ ⚠ _**Declaring clause:** "The locum\'s contract is not in this note, because none of the eleven was dispensed by the locum."_ **Reason: about the thing. ✓**',
  '**b** _(partner)_ _She could check it — the eleven are listed with initials. ⚠ **And she said she would now ask me about the contract**, which means the declaration worked: it told her where the edge of the document is and left her free to ask._',
]));
out.push(S('Stage 4R · Report (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['Mr Shihabi brought two versions of one paragraph, filed four hours apart: eighty words about a water main, in which three things happened — the closure, the cost, and a dispute about who ordered it — and he put all three in. Mrs Rizk asked whether that was in eighty words, and about twenty-six each, which is not enough for any of them. The desk cut the dispute, the cut version is better, and he has been annoyed about that for three weeks. Mrs Tarabishi asked why annoyed if it is better, and because the desk chose and he had not: he had written three strands at twenty-six words because he could not decide, and a cut made by somebody who has not been to the site is a cut made on length. Dr Qabbani asked what he would have done if he had chosen, and then the second version would have forty words on the closure and thirty-five on the cost, and one clause saying the ordering is disputed and is not in this piece. Mrs Oueidat said that clause is the whole difference. Then Mrs Ajouz said something about the examination, because her previous school told her to cover all three points and to be brief about each: the instruction says discuss two of the following three, and she was taught that covering three shows range. It does not — it shows that you did not read the instruction, and she has sat the examination once already and has the band scores. Asked what they said: content, three out of five, with nothing else below four, having written about all three points and about seventy words on each. Mr Zahreddine said so the mark was for choosing, and the mark was for doing what was asked, and what was asked was to choose — three out of five is not a mark for weak English, and nobody at her school could tell her what it was for. Mr Attar asked what you say about the one you leave out, and Mrs Khoury said one clause, with a reason, and then never again: I have not costed the third option, because the second is already cheaper than doing nothing. That is a declared omission and a reader can check it. Mrs Tarabishi offered the alternative — space does not permit a full treatment — which names nothing: a silent omission with a sentence in front of it, and worse than silence, because it tells the reader there is something there and refuses to say what. And Mr Shihabi said the stop is the hard part: one clause and then nothing, because his instinct after naming the dispute is to say a little about it, and the little is the twenty-six words again.']));
out.push(A('Listener task: the partner\'s declaring clause and whether it could be checked. ⚠ **Sort the ten clauses into two piles: reasons about the thing and reasons about the document.** **The second pile is usually three or four and every one of them contains the word _space_, _time_, _length_ or _scope_.** **Read those four aloud and ask what is missing in each.** **Nobody can say.** **Then the practical instruction for the rest of the book: a declaring clause with the word _space_ in it has not declared anything.**'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**✗** _Space does not permit a full treatment._ → **✓** name the thing and give one reason.   **✗** Three strands at twenty-six words each. → **✓** two at forty.   **✗** _I shall confine myself to the two main points._ → **✓** _main_ names nothing.   **✗** Six more words about the thing you have just declared. → **✓** stop.   **✗** Leaving the date out of a dose. → **✓** that is one fact, not three topics.'));
out.push(S('Stage 5 · Read'));
out.push(A('**1** ⚠ **The date on the notice** — _the directorate\'s own notice for 11 June was dated the eleventh_ — **and it is in the second filing only.** **It was in his notebook at six o\'clock and there was no room for it in the first version.**'));
out.push(A('**2** ⚠ **The desk kept the cost and cut the date on the notice.** **He says the desk could not have done otherwise because the dispute was the third of three and the paragraph was long** — **and the desk cut on length, which is the only instrument available to somebody who has not been to the site.** **What survived is the figure anybody can get from the directorate.**'));
out.push(A('**3** ⚠ **One sentence, seventeen words, with nothing attached:** _the order for the work is disputed between the directorate and the municipality and is not in this report._ **And the arithmetic of why: two claims, two sources and the document each side says exists is about fifty words**, and there is no honest version in twenty-six.'));
out.push(S('Stage 6 · Write with help (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**SECOND FILING** _(seventy-six words)_',
  '**Residents of four streets off the Mezzeh highway were given no notice of water-main closures on two of the three mornings in June, and about four hundred households were affected.** ⚠ **The directorate\'s own notice for 11 June was dated the eleventh.** **The work cost an estimated eleven million pounds, a third of the quarterly maintenance budget, and the directorate confirmed the figure on Tuesday.** ⚠ **The order for the work is disputed between the directorate and the municipality and is not in this report.**',
  '**What is in this version and not in the first.** **The date on the notice** — **which is one clause and is the only new fact in any version.**',
]));
out.push(S('Stage 7 · Write freely (sample, 281 words)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**NOTE ON THE OCTOBER DISPENSINGS** _(N. Ajouz · to the regional pharmacist · 22 November)_',
  '**Eleven dispensings in October were made against superseded sheets, and all eleven were made by this dispensary.** **Seven of the eleven were superseded by a dose change and four by a change of prescriber only.** **The seven are the ones that matter and I have listed them separately.**',
  '**How they were found.** **One patient telephoned on 19 October about a dose.** **I read the October file that afternoon and found the other ten.** ⚠ **Nothing in our own system found any of them**, and the sheet-supersession date is printed on every sheet in a box about four millimetres high.',
  '**The seven.** **All seven are repeat prescriptions for patients over seventy.** **Four were dispensed by me and three by the second pharmacist, who left on 14 September** — **and the three were dispensed after she left, from sheets she had filed.** **Two of the seven have been corrected by telephone and five are awaiting an appointment.**',
  '**What has changed since 20 October.** **Every sheet is checked against the prescriber\'s date at the point of dispensing and initialled.** **Between 20 October and the end of November, no dispensing was made against a superseded sheet.** **The check adds about forty seconds to each repeat.**',
  '⚠ **One thing is not in this note.** **The locum agency\'s contract expires in January and I have views about it**, and it is not in this note because none of the eleven was dispensed by the locum. **If the audit raises it, I shall answer then.**',
  '**And the figure I could not get.** **How many dispensaries in this governorate check the supersession date at the point of dispensing.** **I asked the council on 2 November and was told it is not recorded.**',
]));
out.push(A('**281 words, two strands with room, the fact nobody else had in its own clause, the figure she could not get, and one strand declared and dropped in a sentence.** ⚠ **The sentence that earns the band is _nothing in our own system found any of them_.** **Eight words, and it is the only sentence in the note that is about the dispensary rather than about the sheets** — **and it is placed where a reader with four hundred letters a month will reach it.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 7P · Respond (sample slip — band 1)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '⚠ **The reader I am being: the regional pharmacist**, who will decide whether this is an audit matter or a conversation.',
  '**Criterion: two strands with enough room.** _"Seven of the eleven were superseded by a dose change and four by a change of prescriber only."_ **As that reader the split tells me which four I can stop thinking about**, which is the first decision I have to take.',
  '**What that reader would do about it.** ⚠ **Ask for the seven by name and the forty-second check in writing.** **Both are offered.**',
  '⚠ **Not on this slip:** the declared omission about the locum contract is well done and I would not notice it. **As the pharmacist I should simply not have thought about the contract at all.**',
]));
out.push(S('Stage 7R · Share'));
out.push(A('⚠ **Time the stop, because the six words are real and they are the same six every time.** **One student reads the two strands and the declaring clause, then stops** — and somebody adds _although of course the municipality says…_ **Write the addition on the board.** **It is almost always an attempt to be fair to the thing that was left out**, which is why the instinct is hard to break: **the addition is generous and it reopens everything the clause closed.** **Say it once in those terms and the class will hear it in their own mouths for the rest of the book.**'));
out.push(S('Homework (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '_**Three strands became two.** The eleven dispensings and the seven that matter; the locum contract declared and dropped._',
  '⚠ _**Which strand would a stranger have cut?** The seven over seventy, because it has no document attached and reads like an interpretation._ **And it is the one I went and found: nobody told me they were all over seventy, I counted.**',
]));

// ===================== 3.2 =====================
out.push(pb());
out.push(h2('3.2  The criterion you never state'));
out.push(S('Stage 1 · Get ready'));
out.push(A('**a** Open. ⚠ **The room guesses the criterion and the guess is the data, so collect it before any discussion.** **Write the ten sentences and the ten guessed criteria side by side.** **In most classes four or five of the guesses are _cost_ or _time_, whatever the sentence was about**, and that distribution is the finding of the cycle arriving before the dialogue does.'));
out.push(A('**b** Leaves its criterion to the reader: **1** and **4**. ⚠ **Not 2** (states _cheaper_), **not 3** (_overall value_, which states a criterion and the criterion is empty), **not 5** (a decision and not a comparison). **Read 3 aloud and ask what _overall value_ could be checked against.** **Nothing** — and that is the surprise the cycle turns on: **naming your ground badly is worse than not naming it.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 2 · Listen and read'));
out.push(A('**a** **B** — **the reader supplies the criterion they already use, so an unstated comparison is a bet on who is reading.**'));
out.push(A('**b** **1** **Two ways of getting a pharmacy registration recognised**, and the room supplied **time**, because the sentence had two numbers in it — **and Mr Shihabi supplied time because he has a deadline every evening.** **2** ⚠ **Cost. Always cost, and never safety** — **and he wrote _the replacement bolts are M16 and the installed bolts are M12_ for eleven years before anybody told him that three people were reading three different sentences.**'));
out.push(NOTE('⚠ **The demonstration in this cycle is better than the explanation, so let it happen and do not pre-empt it.** **Mr Shihabi supplies _time_ in the dialogue, out loud, and then says why: he has a deadline every evening.** **Do the same thing in the room with Stage 4b, which is built for it: one sentence, two partners with different jobs, and two answers.** **The engineer supplies specification, the journalist supplies time, the grants officer supplies cost, the translator supplies who is at fault.** ⚠ **Collect the ten answers and the class will see that the criterion a reader supplies is a fact about their working week and not about your sentence.** **That is the whole argument for giving both facts for both things**, and no amount of saying it does what ten answers on a board do.'));
out.push(S('Language box · Practise'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**1** _(two routes)_ _The first takes four months and needs a countersignatory for two years; the second takes eleven and needs nobody._',
  '**2** _(two suppliers)_ _The first delivers in nine days and holds no stock; the second in twenty-one and holds four weeks._',
  '**3** _(two dates)_ _The January date falls inside the audit period and needs no notice; the March date falls outside it and needs twenty-eight days._',
  '**4** _(two candidates)_ _One has eleven years in one records office; the other has four years in three._',
  '**5** _(you know your reader and want the bet)_ ⚠ _To the insurer: the installed bolts are M12 and the specified bolts are M16. **The bet is that he supplies cost, which he will, and the cost of replacement is the argument I want him to reach without my making it.**_',
]));
out.push(A('⚠ Mark these on **whether both facts are given for both things.** **A one-sided comparison is the commonest failure and it is not neutral:** _the second route takes eleven months_, **with no figure for the first, reads as a complaint about the second** — **a reader given one number invents the other, and the invented one always flatters whichever side they prefer.** **Item 5 is the only place in the unit where making the bet is allowed**, and it is allowed because the writer has named it.'));
out.push(S('Stage 3 · Practise (sample – Card A)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**A** _(reads)_ _The second route takes eleven months and the first takes four._',
  '**B** I supplied: **time.** A different reader would supply: **dependence — the supervisor.**',
  '**B** Empty criterion: **no.** ⚠ _(the second row)_ _The second supplier offers better overall value._ **Empty: _overall value_, which cannot be checked against a price, a date or a stock level.**',
]));
out.push(S('Stage 3R · Perform – listener task'));
out.push(A('The criteria supplied and the four empty ones. ⚠ **_Overall value_, _more suitable_, _operational standpoint_ and _more efficient_ cannot be checked against anything**, and the finding is that all four were produced by naming a criterion rather than by leaving one out. **Put the four on the board and ask the room to replace each with a fact.** _Better overall value_ **becomes _nine days against twenty-one, and no stock against four weeks_** — **which is longer, and is the only version either reader can use.** ⚠ **Then the warning: the four empty phrases are all B2 habits and all four are praised at B2**, because at B2 naming your ground was the lesson. **Say so, or the class will think they have been taught badly.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 4 · Your turn (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**a** _Both facts for both: "The Friday clinic takes forty-one patients and runs with one pharmacist; the Saturday clinic takes nineteen and runs with two."_ ⚠ _**What it is really comparing:** cost per patient, which is not what either figure says on its own._',
  '**b** _(two partners)_ _The engineer said it compares staffing. The grants officer said it compares demand._ ⚠ _**Two answers, and neither is cost per patient.** **So the sentence is a bet and I have lost it twice in one evening** — and in a note to a donor and a field office at once I cannot afford it._',
]));
out.push(S('Stage 4R · Report (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['Mrs Ajouz brought two ways of getting a pharmacy registration recognised and one sentence: the second route takes eleven months and the first takes four. Mr Shihabi said then the first one is better, which is what everybody says and is not in the sentence — the first takes four months and requires a supervisor who will sign for two years, and the second takes eleven and requires nobody. Mrs Tarabishi said so he supplied time because the sentence had two numbers in it, and Mr Shihabi said he supplied time because he has a deadline every evening. Mrs Khoury said that is the finding, demonstrated by Mr Shihabi on himself: a reader with no stated criterion supplies the one they already use, so an unstated comparison is a bet on who is reading it. Mr Tayara said which pays if you know them, and it pays very well if you know them and loses completely if you do not — and in a document with three readers you do not. Then Mr Zahreddine said what an insurer supplies: cost, always cost, and never safety. Dr Qabbani asked whether that holds even in an engineering opinion, and especially there: the replacement bolts are M16 and the installed bolts are M12 — an engineer reads a specification, an owner reads a delay and an insurer reads a figure, and he wrote that sentence for eleven years before anybody told him that three people were reading three different sentences. Mrs Oueidat said in court the criterion is always supplied by whoever speaks first. Mrs Rizk said so the rule is to state it, and Mrs Khoury said no, and this is the cycle: stating it is the B2 move and it is often worse. The second route is better than the first in terms of overall value states a criterion and the criterion is empty, and a reader cannot check overall value against anything. Mrs Tarabishi asked then what, and the answer is to give both facts for both things and let the criterion be visible without being named: the first takes four months and needs a supervisor for two years; the second takes eleven and needs nobody. The criterion is now in the reader\'s hands and the reader can see what it is. Mr Attar said that is longer, and Mrs Ajouz said it is nineteen words and it is the first version she has written that a second reader could use — her sister asked her which route, was given the two-number sentence, and said take the four months, and she has no supervisor either. And Mr Shihabi said the delivery is the part he will take away: say it once, with no whereas and no on the other hand, because those two words tell a listener which half you prefer, and the whole point of leaving the criterion out is that you have not told them.']));
out.push(A('Listener task: the two different answers the two partners gave. ⚠ **Collect the pairs and count how many students got two different answers.** **It is most of them, and the distribution is the lesson: the criterion follows the reader\'s job with almost no exceptions.** **Then the one case that is worth five minutes — the student whose two partners agreed.** **Either the sentence names its criterion already, or both partners have the same working week**, and the room can usually say which in about four seconds.'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**✗** _Better overall value._ → **✓** nine days against twenty-one.   **✗** _The second takes eleven months._ → **✓** both figures, or the reader invents one.   **✗** _More suitable for our purposes._ → **✓** what purpose, measured how?   **✗** _Whereas the first takes four._ → **✓** _whereas_ names your preference.   **✗** Naming a criterion because B2 said to. → **✓** name a fact instead.'));
out.push(S('Stage 5 · Read'));
out.push(A('**1** ⚠ **For each route: the time, what it asks of the reader, what can go wrong, and how many people her figures rest on.** **What she never says is which is better, or on what ground** — **there is no sentence anywhere in the note naming a criterion.**'));
out.push(A('**2** ⚠ **How many restart route one each year, and how many abandon route two after one failure.** **She asked the council in September and was told neither is compiled.** **She records it because the comparison rests on seven people she happens to know** — _and seven is not a number._'));
out.push(A('**3** ⚠ **Rana has a registered aunt who has worked in the same pharmacy for nineteen years, and the cousin has nobody.** **It appears in the last paragraph, after everything else, and is explicitly not weighed against anything** — **which is the honest shape: one fact outranks the whole comparison and the comparison is still worth writing, because neither reader knew the four months needed a countersignatory at all.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 6 · Write with help (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**THE TWO DATES** _(F. Zahreddine · for the owner and the contractor · 2 December)_',
  '**Striking the scaffold on Saturday costs two days of the sheeting contractor and lets the nine positions be measured on Monday morning.** **Leaving it until the sheeting comes down in January costs nothing and leaves the elevation unloaded for six weeks.**',
  '**What Saturday asks of you.** **A sheeting crew at weekend rates and a decision by Thursday.** ⚠ **The crew must be the same crew that sheeted it** — **if it is not, the scaffold has to be inspected again before anybody goes up, which is another day.**',
  '**What January asks of you.** **Nothing from either of you, and six weeks of an elevation that cannot carry anything.** **Of the four jobs I have seen done this way, three were fine and one found a second fault in January that would have been found in August.**',
]));
out.push(S('Stage 7 · Write freely (sample, 289 words)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**THE TWO ROUTES** _(N. Ajouz · for Rana and for my cousin in Homs · 3 November)_',
  '**Route one takes about four months and needs a registered pharmacist who will countersign your practice record for two years.** **Route two takes about eleven months and needs nobody.** **Both end in the same registration and neither can be begun while the other is open.**',
  '**What route one asks of you.** **Twenty-two months of supervised practice, countersigned monthly.** **The countersignatory must be registered throughout and must not change** — **if they leave or let their registration lapse, the record restarts.** **Of the four people I know who took route one, three finished in four or five months and one restarted in the eighth month and is still going.**',
  '**What route two asks of you.** **Three written examinations, in April, August and December, with a fee each time and no limit on attempts.** **No practice record.** **Of the three people I know who took it, two finished in eleven months and one in twenty-three, having failed the August paper twice.**',
  '**What is the same in both.** **The final fee.** **Two references from outside your own pharmacy.** **A six-week wait at the end that nobody can shorten.**',
  '**What neither route tells you until you are in it.** **Route one\'s countersignatory also decides whether a month counts, and two of the four I know had a month refused.** **Route two\'s April paper is the only one marked externally**, which is why April\'s pass rate is not published.',
  '**The figures I could not get.** **How many restart route one; how many abandon route two after one failure.** ⚠ **I asked the council in September and was told neither is compiled.** **The comparison above rests on seven people I happen to know, and seven is not a number.**',
  '⚠ **And I am not going to tell either of you which to take.** **Rana has a registered aunt in the same pharmacy for nineteen years and my cousin has nobody.**',
]));
out.push(A('**289 words, both facts for both routes, no criterion named anywhere, the size of the evidence declared, the missing figures recorded, and the deciding fact left unweighed.** ⚠ **The sentence that earns the band is _seven is not a number._** **Five words, and they do what a paragraph of hedging would do badly**: they tell both readers exactly how far to push everything above them.'));
