// ===================== 3.3 =====================
out.push(pb());
out.push(h2('3.3  Three photographs, two chosen'));
out.push(S('Stage 1 · Get ready'));
out.push(A('**a** Open. ⚠ **Get the one-sentence answer from four or five students before you confirm anything.** **The answer you want is _choosing_, and about half the room will say _there are three now_, which is not the same thing** — **the number of photographs is not the change; the number you talk about is.**'));
out.push(A('**b** The task asks for **2**. ⚠ **Not 1 or 3**, which describe; **not 4**, which is a preference; **not 5**, which declares the omission and is **wrong here and right in 3.1.** **Read 5 out last and let the room argue about it**, because that argument is the cycle: **the examiner put three pictures on the table and knows exactly what you have left.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 2 · Listen and read'));
out.push(A('**a** **B** — **which two were chosen and on what ground, in one clause**, because the ground is what the remaining fifty seconds has to be about.'));
out.push(A('**b** **1** ⚠ **She described all three, twenty seconds each, and ran out of time before comparing anything.** **The report said: range of vocabulary good; discourse management limited; did not develop a line of argument.** **2** **Three photographs come in and two go out, and the choosing takes about nine seconds** — ⚠ **on what the two together say that neither says alone.**'));
out.push(NOTE('⚠ **The nine seconds is the teachable part and it has a method, which the Student\'s Book gives through an example rather than as a rule, so draw it out in Stage 3.** **Try the pairs, not the photographs.** **A queue and a notice: nobody told them. A queue and a child: it went on all morning. A notice and a child: nothing.** **Usually two of the three pairs work and one does not.** **Make the class do all three pairings out loud for each set before anybody speaks for a minute** — **it takes two minutes and it is the difference between a turn with a subject and a turn that describes.** ⚠ **And name the trap: the strongest single image is the one most likely to clash with its partner rather than add to it**, because it takes up the whole of a reader\'s attention. **The picture editor says so in the Track 3.15 interview and it is the rule to leave the room with.**'));
out.push(NOTE('**Two practical matters about running the timed stages, both of which cost nothing and change the outcome.** ⚠ **One: somebody must hold a watch and say _stop_ at sixty seconds, out loud, in the middle of a sentence.** **A class that is allowed to finish does not learn to finish.** **Do it twice: first time round two or three of ten end on a full stop, second time round most of them.** ⚠ **Two: the thirty-second response is 3.4 and must not be run here**, however naturally the pairs fall into it. **If B answers A in this cycle, 3.4 has nothing left to teach and the class will arrive at it believing they can already do it** — **and the one thing they cannot do is open thirty seconds without mentioning their partner.**'));
out.push(S('Language box · Practise'));
