out.push(S('Stage 4R · Report (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['Mrs Ajouz has done this in the room where it counts, and she described all three: twenty seconds each, and she ran out of time before she had compared anything. The report said range of vocabulary good, discourse management limited, did not develop a line of argument — and she had no line of argument because she had three subjects and a minute. Mr Zahreddine said that is the same arithmetic as the eighty words, and it is exactly the same, and nobody at her school connected the two: she was taught the writing task and the speaking task by two different teachers and neither mentioned that the first decision is identical. Mr Shihabi said a picture desk does this forty times a day — three photographs come in and two go out, and the choosing takes about nine seconds. Dr Qabbani asked on what ground, and on what the two of them together say that neither says alone, which is the whole of it and is not about which photograph is best. Mrs Oueidat asked for one, and he gave it: a queue at a gate, a man reading a notice, and a child asleep on a wall. The queue and the notice together say that nobody told them. The queue and the child together say that it went on a long time. The notice and the child say nothing at all. Mr Tayara said so two of the three pairs work and one does not, and usually two of the three, and finding out which is the nine seconds — a candidate who picks the two best photographs has picked on the wrong thing, because it is the pair that has to do the work. Mrs Rizk said and you say the ground in the first clause, and Mrs Khoury said in one clause, at the start, and then you spend fifty seconds on it: I shall talk about the first and the third, because both show somebody waiting for a decision that is not theirs. Seventeen words, and the rest of the minute now has a subject. Mr Attar asked about the third picture, and nothing: no I shall leave the second aside, no explanation, no apology, because the instruction is to talk about two and saying why you have not chosen the third is a declared omission in a task where the omission is the instruction. Mrs Ajouz said that is the opposite of 3.1, and it is, and the difference is who set the limit: in 3.1 you chose to leave something out and the reader did not know it existed, and here the examiner put three pictures in front of you and knows exactly what you have left. And she said the ending is the thing she will practise — one minute, ending on a full stop, because hers ended on and the third one also shows with the examiner saying thank you over the top of it, and that is on the recording.']));
out.push(A('Listener task: the rejected pair and what it would have been about. ⚠ **Collect the rejected pairs and sort the answers into _nothing_ and _something_.** **A rejection whose answer is _nothing_ was correct.** **A rejection whose answer is _something_ was a choice, and the student should be able to say which of two subjects they preferred and why.** **In most rooms three or four students discover that they rejected a working pair because it contained the photograph they liked least**, which is the opposite of the method and is worth naming.'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**✗** Describing all three. → **✓** two, and nothing about the third.   **✗** _I shall leave the second aside._ → **✓** the examiner knows.   **✗** Choosing the two best frames. → **✓** choose the pair that does the work.   **✗** Still speaking at sixty seconds. → **✓** finish at fifty-four.   **✗** Fifty seconds of description. → **✓** the ground, applied to both.'));
