out.push(S('Stage 4R · Report (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['Mr Tayara has been asked three questions by people who will not meet him: a circular to all heads with three questions and a page and a half — how the ten extra minutes affect teaching time, how many families are affected, and whether the change should be adopted across the governorate. Mrs Rizk said a page and a half for three, and that is five hundred words, and the third question cannot be answered in a hundred and sixty: it is a policy question and he runs one school. Dr Qabbani said so he answered two, and two, and he said which two in the first sentence, and the ground was the thing he had to think about for an evening. Mrs Khoury asked for the first version, and it was: I shall deal with the first and second questions, which are the most important. That is what he wrote at nine o\'clock and it is useless. Mrs Tarabishi said importance is the worst ground there is — it is not a property of the questions at all but an opinion about them, and a reader who disagrees has nothing to disagree with, which means they simply think you chose the easy two. Mr Shihabi said which he did, and Mr Tayara said which he did, and the second version says so without apologising: of the three questions, the first two can be answered from this school\'s own records and the third cannot be answered by a head teacher at all. Mrs Oueidat said that names a property, and Mrs Khoury said it names a property the two chosen questions share and the third does not — answerable from records — and a reader can check it. That is the difference between a ground and a preference: a ground can be wrong. Mrs Ajouz said that in the examination the three points are not questions but statements, and the grounds are the same four: the two that can be tested against something; the two that are about the same thing; the two where you have evidence and the third is opinion; or the two that disagree with each other, which is the strongest and the rarest. Mr Attar asked why that one is strongest, and because an essay about two points that pull in opposite directions has an argument built into it before you write a word, where two points that agree give you four hundred words of saying the same thing twice. Mrs Tarabishi asked about the one left out — one clause, as in 3.1 — and one clause, in the first paragraph, and never again: the third cannot be answered by a head teacher at all is eleven words, and the examiner has the ground and the omission before the end of paragraph one. And Mr Tayara said it has to be said in one breath, which he found out reading it to his wife: the first version had three commas in it and she asked him what he was apologising for.']));
out.push(A('Listener task: the attempt to make the ground wrong, and whether it survived. ⚠ **Count the survivals and the falls.** **About a third fall, and every fall is a ground improved in forty seconds.** **Then the observation that makes the stage worth keeping: a ground that falls over in the room would have fallen over in the examiner\'s head silently, and the candidate would never have known.** **This is the only stage in Book 1 where a student finds out that an argument of theirs does not work while there is still time to change it.**'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**✗** _The most important._ → **✓** a property that could be wrong.   **✗** _In the time available._ → **✓** about the page, not the points.   **✗** Three commas in the ground. → **✓** one breath.   **✗** Inventing a property when all three are testable. → **✓** admit the preference in four words.   **✗** Mentioning the third point in paragraph two. → **✓** one clause, and never again.'));
