out.push(S('Stage 4R · Report (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['Two essays on the three points about expertise. Mrs Ajouz took the first and the third — the best work looks easy, and training makes a skill visible before it makes it automatic — and by the third paragraph she found that the second one explains the first. Mrs Tarabishi took the first and the second — looks easy, and experts misjudge their own competence — and hers turned into an essay about evidence. Dr Qabbani said from the same three sentences, and from the same three sentences, and neither of them is wrong: Mrs Tarabishi\'s is about why nobody can assess themselves and Mrs Ajouz\'s is about why the method has to be shown first. Mrs Khoury said that is the point of the cycle — the mark is for what you do with the pair and not for which pair you took, and two different pairs produce two different essays and both can be band five. Mr Shihabi asked what happens if you take the two that agree, and Mrs Ajouz said then you write four hundred words twice, which is what she did in the examination with three: the pair that argues is the one that writes itself. Mr Zahreddine then asked about a paragraph in Mrs Ajouz\'s essay, because the second one says almost nothing new. She said it says that visible effort is not rewarded either, and he said which he already believed, and the paragraph is still doing something, because without it the third paragraph has nothing to stand on — and he has written structures like that and never written a paragraph like that. Mrs Khoury said it is the load-bearing paragraph and it is Unit 23 in the second book: a paragraph whose only job is to let the next one work is not padding, and the test is whether the next paragraph survives its removal. Mr Tayara asked about the conclusion, and it follows from the two paragraphs and adds one thing — what follows is practical rather than a matter of opinion: the stage of visible method is a condition to be left and not a fault to be avoided. The one thing is practical rather than a matter of opinion, and nothing above it said that. Mrs Oueidat offered the alternative, in conclusion both points are clearly very important, which adds nothing and follows from nothing: the sentence a candidate writes when the four hundred words have not decided anything, and an examiner has read it eleven times that morning. Mr Attar asked about the third point — one clause in paragraph one and nothing after — and nothing after, and Mrs Ajouz wanted to put something: her second paragraph had as I said, I am not dealing with the second point in it, and she took it out at the end. And Mrs Tarabishi said the conclusion goes down and not up: she read hers aloud and the last four words went up, as though she were asking whether it would do, which is exactly what she was doing.']));
out.push(A('Listener task: what the partner\'s essay is going to be about, in one sentence. ⚠ **Collect the pairs of sentences and check that each pair is genuinely different.** **Where two students with different pairs produce the same sentence, one of them has not used their pair** — **and where a student cannot produce a sentence at all, their pair agrees with itself.** **Both findings are available in ninety seconds and both save an essay.** **Then the instruction for the homework: find the person with the other pair and read each other\'s.** **Two people in the dialogue did it by accident and it is the best thing in the cycle.**'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**✗** _In conclusion, both points are important._ → **✓** what follows, and one addition.   **✗** _To sum up, I have discussed two points._ → **✓** metatext: cover it and nothing is unknown.   **✗** _As I said, I am not dealing with the second point._ → **✓** one clause in paragraph one, and never again.   **✗** Deleting the paragraph that says nothing new. → **✓** test by removal, not by novelty.   **✗** A pair that agrees. → **✓** four hundred words twice.'));
out.push(S('Stage 5 · Read'));
out.push(A('**1** ⚠ **Essay One: _the two that explain each other_, and the second is _a claim about psychology that neither of them needs_.** **Essay Two: _the two that can be tested against something_, and the third is _a claim about teaching that this essay does not need_.** **Both grounds are properties and both could be wrong**, and neither writer mentions the omitted point again until Essay Two\'s last clause.'));
out.push(A('**2** ⚠ **The second paragraph is load-bearing.** **It says that visible effort is not rewarded either** — which most readers accept — **and without it the third paragraph\'s claim that the visible stage is unavoidable has nothing to stand on**: the reader would still believe there is a third option in which effort is shown and rewarded.'));
out.push(A('**3** ⚠ **It is not a fault and it is the strongest available ending.** **The last clause is _which is a claim about training, which is where the third point was and why I have not needed it_** — **so the omission has been justified by the essay instead of by the first paragraph.** **An argument that arrives at the point it set aside and says so has shown that the point was not needed**, which is a stronger claim than any ground in a first paragraph.'));
out.push(S('Stage 6 · Write with help (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**Of the three claims, the second and the third are the two that cannot both be true, and the first is a claim about readers that neither of them needs.** **If references tell a panel nothing, and a panel assesses only what it is shown, then the panel is being asked to work from a set it has itself restricted.**',
  '**That references tell a panel nothing is half true and the wrong half is usually quoted.** Nobody writes an honest unfavourable reference and everybody knows it, so a favourable one carries no information. ⚠ **But the claim assumes that panels read references for information, and they do not** — **they read them for the one line that is missing**, and an absence is information of a kind.',
]));
