out.push(SAMPLE(['Two readings of one essay. Asked which reader is right, Mr Shihabi said the second — and the second reader is the less useful of the two, which is the awkward part. Mrs Tarabishi asked why less useful, since he is correct, and because he says there is nothing to add and there is one thing to add, and it is in her own last paragraph: a reader who approves has stopped reading at the same point as a reader who objects. Dr Qabbani said so the first reader is simply wrong, and Mrs Khoury said the first reader is wrong about the instruction and right about something else, which is worth four minutes — he has noticed that the essay is about the third point, and he has read it as an omission because the essay never admits it. Mr Tayara said so the fault is hers after all, and it is not a fault: it is nine words she has not written — which is where the third point was and why I have not needed it. Her argument arrives at the thing she set aside and she does not say so, and the one reader who noticed concluded she had forgotten it. Mrs Ajouz said that in her examination nobody told her anything at all, she had a number, and Mrs Oueidat said that is the whole reason this cycle exists: an examiner gives a band and a colleague gives an opinion, and neither of them tells you the nine words. Mrs Rizk asked what you actually say to her, and Mr Zahreddine said read her her own last clause and ask what the third point was, which is the whole mediation. Mrs Tarabishi asked whether that is not simply telling her to add nine words, and no, because then they are his nine words: read the clause, ask the question, and the nine words will be hers and she will remember writing them. Mr Attar said that is the same move as 1.7, and Mrs Khoury said it is exactly the same move and the class should notice — say what is correct, show the writer their own sentence, ask one question, stop. Three units and three cycles and it has not changed. Mrs Ajouz asked whether you tell the first reader he has misread the instruction, and Mrs Oueidat said not unless he asks: he is not the person who has to write anything, and a mediator who corrects the reader has spent the cycle on the easier of the two people in front of them. And Mr Shihabi said the thing he will take away is that a reader who praises you is as little use as one who objects — both of them stopped at the same place, and the only reader who was any use is the one who noticed something and got the reason wrong.']));
out.push(A('Listener task: the student\'s own nine words, or the line saying what their essay is about instead. ⚠ **Collect both groups and count them.** **Most essays arrive at the omitted point and most writers have not noticed** — **which is not a coincidence: a set of three points chosen by an examiner is a set in which any two imply something about the third.** **Say that once, because it is the most useful thing a candidate can know about this task.** **Then the second group: a student whose essay does not arrive at the third point should check that their line about what the essay is about is not the ground from paragraph one.** **If it is, they have written the first paragraph twice.**'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**✗** _Add nine words about the third point._ → **✓** then they are your nine words.   **✗** Telling the first reader he has misread the instruction. → **✓** not unless he asks.   **✗** _There is nothing to add._ → **✓** correct, and there was one thing.   **✗** Filling the four seconds. → **✓** the answer is in front of her.   **✗** Mediating the reader instead of the writer. → **✓** the easier of the two people.'));
